<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Potsdam Agreement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> Agreement was signed on 1 August 1945 at the end of the Potsdam Conference between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the United Kingdom. It set out the principles of the Allied occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, including demilitarisation, denazification, democratisation, decentralisation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>, and approved the transfer of German populations from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. It also placed the lands east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oder-neisse-line">Oder–Neisse line</a> under Polish and Soviet administration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/potsdam-agreement.jpg" alt="Potsdam Agreement" width="320" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: US Government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clement_Attlee,_Harry_S._Truman,_and_Joseph_Stalin_at_the_Potsdam_Conference_1945,_NARA_(198950).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the end of the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> assumed supreme authority over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> by the Berlin Declaration of 5 June 1945. The Potsdam Conference ran from 17 July to 2 August 1945 and was attended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>, President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-s-truman">Harry S. Truman</a> and Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-attlee">Clement Attlee</a>, who replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> after the British general election. At the conference the Western Allies were presented with Stalin's fait accompli awarding Soviet-occupied Poland the river Oder as its western border. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was not invited, and its provisional government accepted a seat on the Council of Foreign Ministers with the reservation that it would not accept in advance any commitment to a central German government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The agreement divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> into four occupation zones, with the commanders-in-chief exercising authority within their zones and jointly through the Allied Control Council for Germany as a whole. Its principles were democratisation, treatment of Germany as a single unit, disarmament and demilitarisation, and the removal of Nazi influence. Civilian heavy industry with war potential was to be reduced or destroyed and the economy turned towards agriculture and light industry. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> from its own zone, and 10 per cent of the western zones' unnecessary industrial capacity was to be transferred to it. All but thirty German submarines were to be sunk and the rest of the navy divided equally.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the United Kingdom promised to support the transfer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Poland's provisional western border was set at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oder-neisse-line">Oder–Neisse line</a>, with the final delimitation awaiting a peace settlement. The three governments also recognised that German populations remaining in Poland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> would have to be transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and agreed that this should be done in an orderly and humane manner. Other clauses concerned the trial of war criminals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, peace treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the withdrawal of troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Allied Control Council was constituted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 30 July 1945 to carry out the Four Ds of denazification, demilitarisation, democratisation and decentralisation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> soon led to German rearmament, and dismantling stopped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> in 1951.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> resisted implementing the agreement in its zone and refused to resettle expelled Germans. The transfer clause was used to legitimise the expulsion of Germans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and the lands placed under Polish administration, and expulsions also occurred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. The agreement was a communiqué and not a peace treaty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oder-neisse-line">Oder–Neisse line</a> became Poland's permanent border only in the 1990 German–Polish Border Treaty, and the Treaty on the Final Settlement with Respect to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> of 12 September 1990 superseded the agreement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Potsdam Agreement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Allied Control Council coordinated policy under the <b>Potsdam Agreement</b>: denazification, demilitarisation, democratisation and reparations.</blockquote><a href="/state/allied-occupied-germany">Allied-occupied Germany</a></li></ul></section>
